<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">15 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">10 events that happened on 15 January, from 69 to 1973.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/galba" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/69">69 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,957 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Galba murdered in the Roman Forum</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Praetorians loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a> killed the elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> after just seven months as emperor. Otho, then Vitellius and finally Vespasian took power in the turmoil known as the Year of the Four Emperors.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1559">1559 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">467 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Elizabeth I is crowned at Westminster Abbey</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Elizabeth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and Anne Boleyn, was crowned on a date chosen by her astrologer John Dee. Her 44-year reign saw a Protestant settlement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Armada</a>'s defeat and a flowering of literature.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1582">1582 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">444 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Truce of Jam Zapolski ends Russia's war with Poland-Lithuania</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Ivan the Terrible gave up his conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> to Stefan Batory's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The truce ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s long attempt to win a foothold on the Baltic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1759">1759 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">267 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">British Museum opens to the public</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Founded on the collections of the physician Sir Hans Sloane, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> opened in Montagu House, Bloomsbury. It was one of the first national museums open to all 'studious and curious persons'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1777">1777 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">249 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vermont declares itself an independent republic</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Settlers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire-grants">New Hampshire Grants</a> declared independence as New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, renamed Vermont that summer. The republic outlawed adult <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as the 14th state in 1791.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1870">1870 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">156 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Thomas Nast's cartoon links the Democrats with a donkey</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Nast's cartoon 'A Live Jackass Kicking a Dead Lion' in Harper's Weekly portrayed anti-war Democrats as a donkey. The donkey later became the accepted symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1919">1919 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">107 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht murdered in Berlin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a>, the two communist leaders were seized and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> soldiers. The murders deepened the bitter split on the German left.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1929">1929 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">97 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Martin Luther King Jr. is born in Atlanta</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Born Michael King Jr., the son of a Baptist minister, he became the leading voice of the American civil rights movement. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1964 and was assassinated in 1968.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1943">1943 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">83 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Pentagon is completed in Arlington, Virginia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Built in just 16 months, the five-sided headquarters of the US War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> was dedicated. With some 6.5 million square feet of floor space, it was the world's largest office building.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1973">1973 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">53 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nixon suspends offensive action against North Vietnam</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Citing progress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> peace talks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> halted US bombing and mining of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a>, signed twelve days later, ended direct American involvement in the war.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li></ol>
